  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1235 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA break down of northwesterly flow is expected as we moved into\nthe weekend as model agreement see the full exit of the two upper-\nlevel low pressure systems. Behind this flow, present agreement\ndoes see a new trough dig south out of Western Canada and then\nbrush the Northern Panhandles Saturday evening. At this time,\npresent ensembles are still expecting this brushing not result in\nany precipitation with chances still sitting below 10 percent.\nHowever, there are still a few mid to long-range models that still\nsee some potential for an isolated shower or two brushing the far\nnorth. Regardless, the bigger concern with this passage continues\nto focused on the front and the winds that are expected to follow.\nAt this time present model agreement continue to see the\ndescendant of the Polar Jet and a decent jet streak alongside this\ntrough. This jet streak looks to bring 500mb winds upwards of 70kt\nand 700mb winds peaking around 50kts, which can easily translated\nto surface wind gusts upwards of 60 mph. However, these winds may\nhave difficult time transitioning down to the surface given\ncurrent timing has placed them arriving behind the front Saturday\nnight. Still, present ensembles are starting to like our chances\nwith much of the Western and Northern Panhandles now having a 20\nto 50 percent chance of seeing wind gust of 60 mph or greater.\nBeyond that, cooler and quieter conditions will look to fill in\nfor Sunday with high temperatures looking to return to the 50s.\nFor next week, most models agree to holding on to dry and warmer\nconditions through Wednesday with highs returning to the 60s.\nHowever, there have been hints of another system pushing through\nto close out next week, but confidence in it is low at this time.",
